        'moon.faq.a16': 'This is called "tidal locking": the Moon\'s rotation period (27.3 days) equals its orbital period around Earth. As a result the same hemisphere always faces Earth, while the opposite side is called the "far side" and was first photographed in 1959 by Soviet probe Luna 3.',
        'moon.faq.q17': 'How does the Moon affect tides?',
        'moon.faq.a17': 'Moon gravity pulls ocean water, causing a bulge on the Moon-facing side (high tide) and another on the opposite side due to inertia. Between them are low tides. Roughly two highs and two lows occur daily. When Moon aligns with the Sun (new or full moon), tides reach maximum — "spring tides".',
        'moon.faq.q18': 'Do lunar phases affect sleep and mood?',
        'moon.faq.a18': 'Limited scientific studies suggest some people experience sleep about 20–30 minutes shorter during full moon nights, possibly due to increased sky brightness. The effect is small and varies between individuals; there is no conclusive evidence that the Moon affects mood or behavior generally.',
        'moon.faq.q19': 'Why does the Moon sometimes look yellow or red?',
        'moon.faq.a19': "When the Moon is near the horizon, its light passes through a thicker slice of atmosphere. Blue wavelengths scatter away, leaving red and yellow to reach the eye. This is the same effect that colors sunrise and sunset. Once high in the sky the Moon returns to its silvery-white color.",
        'moon.faq.q20': 'What are a "Blue Moon" and "Supermoon"?',
        'moon.faq.a20': 'Blue Moon: the second full moon in the same Gregorian month; occurs about every 2.7 years (it is not actually blue). Supermoon: a full moon coinciding with the Moon\'s closest point to Earth (perigee), making it appear ~14% larger and ~30% brighter than an average full moon.',
        // ===== Dynamic answer templates (placeholders: {city} {phaseIcon} {phaseName} {illum} {age} {date} {hijri} {days} {time} {distance}) =====
