        'hijri_ny.faq_q5': 'What is the occasion of the Hijri New Year?',
        'hijri_ny.faq_a5': 'The Hijri calendar begins from the migration (Hijrah) of the Prophet Muhammad ﻺ from Mecca to Medina. This dating was adopted by Caliph Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him).',
        'hijri_ny.faq_q6': 'What is the month of Muharram?',
        'hijri_ny.faq_a6': 'Muharram is the first month of the Hijri year and one of the four sacred months. Fasting is recommended during it, especially on the Day of Ashura.',
        'hijri_ny.faq_q7': 'What is the Day of Ashura?',
        'hijri_ny.faq_a7': 'Ashura is the tenth day of Muharram. Fasting on it is a confirmed Sunnah because on this day Allah saved Moses (peace be upon him) and his people from Pharaoh.',
        'hijri_ny.faq_q8': 'Do Muslims celebrate the Hijri New Year?',
        'hijri_ny.faq_a8': 'The Hijri New Year is an occasion for reflection and self-review rather than a religious festival, though many Muslims use it to remember the Prophetic migration.',
        'hijri_ny.faq_q9': 'How many days are there in a Hijri year?',
        'hijri_ny.faq_a9': 'The lunar Hijri year is about 354 days, which is 10 or 11 days shorter than the Gregorian solar year.',
        'hijri_ny.faq_q10': 'Why do Islamic occasions shift each Gregorian year?',
        'hijri_ny.faq_a10': 'Because the Hijri year is lunar and shorter than the solar Gregorian year, Islamic occasions advance by 10–11 days every Gregorian year.',
        'hijri_ny.info_title': 'About the Hijri New Year',
        'hijri_ny.info_h1': 'Start of the Islamic Calendar',
        'hijri_ny.info_h1_body': 'The Hijri calendar is a lunar calendar officially adopted by Muslims during the reign of Caliph Umar ibn al-Khattab. Its starting point is the year of the Prophet Muhammad’s migration from Mecca to Medina, and it consists of 12 lunar months.',
        'hijri_ny.info_h2': 'Muharram and the Sacred Months',
        'hijri_ny.info_h2_body': 'Muharram is one of the four sacred months (Dhul-Qi‘dah, Dhul-Hijjah, Muharram, and Rajab). Islam honored it as the Arabs had before, and avoiding injustice and forbidden acts during it is especially emphasized.',
        'hijri_ny.info_h3': 'The Day of Ashura',
        'hijri_ny.info_h3_body': 'The tenth of Muharram is a virtuous day; the Prophet ﻺ fasted it in gratitude to Allah for saving Moses (peace be upon him). Fasting it together with the ninth (Tasu‘a) is recommended to differ from the People of the Book.',
