        // Current phase insight — 8 phases × 3 facets (visual / visibility / about)
        'moon.insight.new.visual': "The Moon is not visible tonight — it lies between Earth and the Sun, with its near side in shadow.",
        'moon.insight.new.visibility': 'The Moon cannot be seen during the New Moon phase, even with a telescope; it will reappear as a thin crescent in the western sky a day or two later.',
        'moon.insight.new.about': "The New Moon marks the start of the lunar month: the moment when the Moon lies between Earth and the Sun, so its near side is unlit.",
        'moon.insight.waxing_crescent.visual': 'The Moon appears as a thin crescent low in the western sky shortly after sunset.',
        'moon.insight.waxing_crescent.visibility': 'Visible for a short window (~1–2 hours) before setting, and needs a clear, unobstructed horizon.',
        'moon.insight.waxing_crescent.about': 'The Waxing Crescent is the first phase after the New Moon, when a small lit sliver begins to grow day by day.',
        'moon.insight.first_quarter.visual': 'The Moon shows as a bright half-disk, rising in the afternoon and staying up until around midnight.',
        'moon.insight.first_quarter.visibility': 'Easily visible for most of the evening, from sunset until roughly midnight.',
        'moon.insight.first_quarter.about': 'The First Quarter is the halfway mark between New Moon and Full Moon, with roughly 50% illumination.',
        'moon.insight.waxing_gibbous.visual': 'The waxing gibbous Moon appears clearly bright in the sky, typically visible from late afternoon until past midnight.',
        'moon.insight.waxing_gibbous.visibility': 'Easily seen during the evening; may occasionally be spotted in daytime before sunset.',
        'moon.insight.waxing_gibbous.about': 'The Waxing Gibbous is the phase between First Quarter and Full Moon, with illumination between 51% and 99%.',
        'moon.insight.full.visual': 'The Moon appears as a fully-lit disk, opposite the Sun in the sky.',
        'moon.insight.full.visibility': 'Easily visible all night, from moonrise at sunset to moonset at sunrise — the brightest phase of all.',
        'moon.insight.full.about': 'The Full Moon is when illumination reaches 100%, occurring when Earth sits between the Moon and the Sun.',
        'moon.insight.waning_gibbous.visual': 'The Moon appears as a bright bulging shape, rising after midnight and climbing high by dawn.',
        'moon.insight.waning_gibbous.visibility': 'Visible from midnight through after sunrise, often still seen in the morning sky.',
        'moon.insight.waning_gibbous.about': 'The Waning Gibbous is the phase right after Full Moon, with illumination dropping from 99% back to 51%.',
        'moon.insight.last_quarter.visual': 'The Moon shows as a half-disk — this time its left half is the illuminated one.',
        'moon.insight.last_quarter.visibility': 'Visible from midnight through after sunrise.',
        'moon.insight.last_quarter.about': 'The Last Quarter is the halfway mark between Full Moon and the next New Moon, at roughly 50% illumination.',
        'moon.insight.waning_crescent.visual': 'The Moon appears as a thin crescent low in the eastern sky before sunrise.',
        'moon.insight.waning_crescent.visibility': 'Visible briefly before dawn, and needs a clear, unobstructed eastern horizon.',
        'moon.insight.waning_crescent.about': 'The Waning Crescent is the last phase before returning to New Moon — a thin crescent that shrinks day by day.',
        'moon.edu_title': 'Understanding Moon Phases',
        'moon.edu_short_p1': 'A lunar cycle takes about 29.5 days, passing through eight phases: New Moon, Waxing Crescent, First Quarter, Waxing Gibbous, Full Moon, Waning Gibbous, Last Quarter, and Waning Crescent.',
        'moon.edu_short_p2': 'Moonrise and moonset times vary from city to city based on longitude and timezone — that\'s why this page shows times in your local timezone.',
        'moon.edu_read_more': 'Read more about moon phases in your city',
        'moon.edu_phases_title': 'The Lunar Cycle and the Eight Phases',
        'moon.edu_phases_1': 'The Moon\u2019s orbit around the Earth takes about 29.5 days — known as the lunar (synodic) month. During this time the Moon passes through eight successive phases, from New Moon through Waning Crescent, before repeating the cycle.',
        'moon.edu_phases_2': 'A New Moon is the moment the Moon lies between the Sun and Earth; the side facing Earth is completely dark (0% illumination). The Moon is not visible then because it rises and sets with the Sun.',
        'moon.edu_phases_3': 'One or two days after the New Moon, the Waxing Crescent begins to appear as a slender arc on the western horizon shortly after sunset. Illumination gradually increases from about 1% to 49%.',
        'moon.edu_phases_4': 'At 50% illumination, the Moon reaches the First Quarter and appears as a half-disc. Then comes the Waxing Gibbous phase (51% to 99%), followed by the Full Moon at 100% illumination.',
        'moon.edu_phases_5': 'The Full Moon rises at sunset and sets at sunrise. After the Full Moon, illumination decreases through the Waning Gibbous, Last Quarter, and Waning Crescent phases, until the Moon returns to a New Moon again.',
        'moon.edu_city_title': 'Why do moon times differ from one city to another?',
        'moon.edu_city_1': 'Moonrise and moonset depend primarily on a city\u2019s longitude. Since Earth rotates once every 24 hours, easterly cities see moonrise before westerly ones. The gap between Earth\u2019s far east and far west can reach 12 hours.',
        'moon.edu_city_2': 'Latitude also affects the direction of moonrise (northeast vs. southeast) and how long the Moon stays in the sky. That is why moon times in Mecca differ from Riyadh, Dubai, Cairo, London and Jakarta.',
        'moon.edu_islam_title': 'The Islamic Significance of the Lunar Cycle',
        'moon.edu_islam_1': 'The Hijri (Islamic) calendar is entirely based on the lunar cycle. Each Hijri month begins with the sighting of the crescent and lasts 29 or 30 days. This lunar cycle determines the dates of the major observances of Islam:',
        'moon.edu_islam_ramadan': 'Ramadan: fasting begins with the sighting of the Ramadan crescent after sunset on the 29th of Shaban.',
        'moon.edu_islam_fitr': 'Eid al-Fitr: begins with the sighting of the Shawwal crescent after Ramadan.',
        'moon.edu_islam_adha': 'Hajj and Eid al-Adha: determined by the Dhu al-Hijjah crescent.',
        'moon.edu_islam_qadr': 'Laylat al-Qadr (Night of Power): in the last ten nights of Ramadan, most often the 27th.',
        'moon.edu_islam_2': 'Naked-eye sighting of the crescent is required by many jurists as the start of a Hijri month. The crescent must typically be at least about 15 hours old at sunset, and high enough above the horizon to be visible.',
